SECTION 1 — DEFINITIONS
================================================================================

1.1  "Software" means the original code and materials in the
     janczakb/filament-flex-fields package, including PHP, Blade, CSS, JavaScript,
     assets, configuration, documentation, and modifications of those materials.
     Third-party components retain their own licenses under Section 6.4.
     Other packages, including Flex Forms, are not included merely because they
     integrate with the Software or share its author or branding.

1.2  "You" or "Licensee" means the named natural person or single legal entity
     exercising the grant. A sole proprietor is the named natural person
     trading under a business name; several trading names do not create several
     licensees. An incorporated company is distinct from its founders, employees,
     shareholders, clients, parent, subsidiaries, and sister companies.

1.3  "Official Distribution Channels" means the Copyright Holder's official
     GitHub repository, its official Packagist listing, and other channels
     expressly authorized by the Copyright Holder. Obtaining the Software from
     those channels is not redistribution by the recipient. Downloading a public
     package, including through Composer, does not itself confer commercial rights.

1.4  "Authorized Personnel" means employees, individual contractors, agencies,
     and hosting/build/service providers accessing the Software solely to develop,
     maintain, secure, or operate a permitted application on Your behalf. They
     must be bound by employment duties or contractual obligations protecting
     the Software against unauthorized use or disclosure. They receive no right
     to use Your license for their own or other clients' products. You remain
     responsible for their compliance within the access You authorize.

1.5  "Software Transfer" means providing or making available a copy of the
     Software, modified or unmodified, to a party outside the applicable grant,
     including in source, archives, vendor directories, repositories, registries,
     installers, containers, virtual machines, binaries, or deployment artifacts.
     A copy need not be standalone, readily extractable, or editable to count.
     A contractual promise not to extract a delivered copy does not prevent a
     Software Transfer. The following are limited permitted exceptions:
     (a) private access and copies for Authorized Personnel, solely for the
         licensed use, with no independent reuse or distribution rights;
     (b) private repositories, internal package caches or forks, CI artifacts,
         backups, and deployment copies accessible only within the applicable
         grant, including Covered Entities under Enterprise Group;
     (c) Runtime Access and Browser Assets under Section 1.6;
     (d) contributions to the Copyright Holder under Section 4;
     (e) copies expressly authorized by a Custom agreement under Section 5.5.
     Public forks, mirrors, customer source access, and customer downloads are
     not permitted merely because the upstream package is publicly available.

1.6  "Runtime Access" means using an application's interface or API without
     receiving its server-side Software, installation artifacts, or rights to
     independently install, maintain, copy, distribute, or sublicense the Software.
     Application administration, tenant settings, and data exports alone are
     Runtime Access; access to server files, vendor directories, repositories,
     container images, or reusable Software code is not.
     "Browser Assets" means HTML, CSS, JavaScript, fonts, and other assets
     ordinarily delivered to a browser to operate the permitted application,
     including its hosted forms or widgets embedded on another website. Serving,
     caching, or publishing those necessary assets through a CDN or a build step
     such as `php artisan filament:assets` is allowed. This exception does not
     authorize an SDK, component download, public source mirror, or asset bundle
     offered for independent reuse. User data, filled forms, ordinary generated
     documents, and configurations containing no Software code are not Software.

1.7  "Permitted Free Use" means the uses below, subject to Section 3:
     (a) Personal, educational, research, or internal operational use by one
         person or entity. Internal use includes staff administration, managing
         Your own goods/services, and incidental customer/supplier portals for
         those operations, provided You are not supplying the application itself
         as a Commercial Product. A staff-only back office that administers an
         otherwise independently functioning business is internal use; components
         forming or configuring the software service sold to users are not made
         internal merely by hiding them behind a staff login.
     (b) Bespoke work-for-hire for a Distinct Client Engagement, where You remain
         the responsible licensee/operator, the client receives Runtime Access
         only, the application serves that client's own internal operations
         described in (a), and the client does not offer it as a software product
         or service to others. Each engagement must independently qualify; there
         is no agency-wide right to sell copies or productized installations.
     (c) Private development, testing, and evaluation, including a non-production
         demonstration, of a prospective Commercial Product. This does not cover
         live customer operations, a paid pilot, or a production launch. Obtain
         the appropriate commercial grant before such use begins.
     Earning revenue from Your ordinary business or charging for bespoke labor
     does not alone disqualify (a) or (b). Conversely, calling a hosted software
     service "consulting," "internal," or "free" does not make it free use when
     it is in substance a Commercial Product. Section 5.6 explains client-owned
     applications and direct licensing without Software Transfer by the agency.

1.8  "Distinct Client Engagement" means an individually identified client and
     written scope of bespoke work, documented before production deployment.
     Several projects may be one engagement only if that written scope covers
     them. Each client's use must satisfy Section 1.7(b), irrespective of how
     many engagements You perform. Repeated installation or sale of Your own
     product is not bespoke work-for-hire for this purpose.

1.9  "Commercial Product" means an application or software service supplied to
     third parties for fees, subscriptions, licensing revenue, advertising,
     bundled consideration, or another commercial benefit, where the Software
     is integrated into that product's functionality. It includes paid SaaS,
     monetized platforms, freemium products, customer-facing software tools,
     and hosted form builders. No minimum revenue, user count, tenant count,
     percentage of Software code, or visibility threshold applies.
     An ordinary business website, shop, booking/contact form, or internal
     portal is not a Commercial Product merely because it supports selling
     Your own non-software goods or services, if Section 1.7(a) otherwise applies.

1.10 "Product" means one identifiable application or service with a coherent
     purpose and product identity, owned by its licensee and offered/operated
     as one product. It includes versions, upgrades, language editions, pricing
     tiers, feature modules, regional deployments, disaster recovery, and
     dedicated tenant instances of that same product. Domains, servers,
     repositories, or databases alone do not determine product count.
     A separately offered application with an independent purpose or product
     identity is a separate Product, even if it shares code or is sold in a
     bundle. A rebrand replacing an existing name is not a new Product; an
     independently offered fork or second product is. Cosmetic tenant branding
     under Section 5.1 is not a separate Product or OEM right.

1.11 "Hosted Use" means operation by You or Authorized Personnel on infrastructure
     under Your operational control, with customers receiving Runtime Access
     only. Commercial cloud hosting and dedicated instances in Your provider
     account qualify. Customer-owned servers, customer cloud accounts/VPCs, or
     delivered customer appliances are Customer Installations when a copy is
     placed there for that customer to possess, administer, or independently
     operate. A managed-service contract, encryption, or retained vendor admin
     access does not turn that installation into Hosted Use. Section 5.6 permits
     a contractor to administer its client's directly licensed own application.

1.12 "Customer Installation" means delivery or deployment of Your product with
     the Software to an external customer's infrastructure or control, including
     on-premises, customer-cloud, self-hosted, downloadable, or offline editions.
     "Redistribution" includes Software Transfer as part of any application,
     kit, plugin, library, or other deliverable, whether sold or given away.
     "OEM" means embedding or supplying the Software in a product for another
     business to distribute, install, rebrand, or sublicense. A sales/referral
     agent arranging Your direct SaaS contracts, without such rights, is not OEM.

1.13 "Derivative Work" means a modification or adaptation of the Software or
     incorporation of its protected code. Integration alone does not transfer
     ownership of Your independently written application to the Copyright Holder.
     "Bundled Product" means an application, library, kit, template, starter,
     boilerplate, or other offering containing or commercially requiring the
     Software. A dependency declaration is not itself a copy of Software, but
     omitting vendor files does not remove the Custom requirement for selling
     a starter or distributable product designed around the Software.

1.14 "Covered Entities" under Enterprise Group means the named parent legal
     entity and subsidiaries in which it owns, directly or through wholly owned
     subsidiaries, 100% of the ownership interests and voting rights, for as
     long as that relationship continues. There is no subsidiary or team count
     limit. Majority ownership, common founders, shared investors, joint ventures,
     franchises, and contractual control alone do not qualify. The named parent's
     own parent or sister companies are not covered unless within this definition
     under the parent actually named in the License Confirmation.

1.15 "License Confirmation" means an email or instrument issued or expressly
     authenticated by the Copyright Holder identifying the licensee, plan,
     effective date, applicable License version, price/currency, payment reference,
     and scope, including the named Product for Single Product or named parent
     for Enterprise Group. A payment receipt alone is not a grant unless it
     expressly contains that authenticated confirmation. A "Custom agreement"
     must expressly identify every additional or varied right it grants.

