SECTION 7 — BREACH, TERMINATION, AND SURVIVAL
================================================================================

7.1  Out-of-scope use is never authorized merely because another use is licensed.
     You must stop unauthorized copying, distribution, or use immediately.
     Subject to mandatory law, the Copyright Holder may terminate the affected
     grant by written notice for an unremedied material breach after thirty (30)
     calendar days' written notice describing it and an opportunity to cure.
     Deliberate unauthorized redistribution, fraudulent licensing information,
     or repeated material breach after a prior cure may justify immediate written
     termination. No cure period grants temporary rights for the disputed use.

7.2  A late payment or administrative error that can be remedied is subject to
     the cure process. A minor breach does not automatically extinguish all
     perpetual rights. For separate identifiable grants, termination affects the
     breached grant, not unrelated compliant grants, unless those grants are
     themselves implicated in the breach.

7.3  After termination, cease use under the terminated grant and remove its
     Software copies, including working copies and deployment artifacts. One
     inaccessible archival copy may be retained where legally required, and
     immutable backups may expire through their ordinary retention cycle if
     not restored to active use. Any independently valid grant remains effective
     for its own scope. Confirm compliance reasonably upon written request.
     Custom downstream treatment follows its express agreement; Standard Plans
     do not create downstream sublicenses that survive independently.

7.4  Rights/remedies accrued before termination, Section 4 contribution grants,
     Section 6 ownership/notices, Sections 8–10 as applicable, and obligations
     necessary to enforce scope and termination survive. Unauthorized use may
     support contractual or copyright remedies to the extent available under
